$/={battery_comparison:[{Chemistry:"LFP",Energy density:"Lower than NMC; IEA says LFP packs are about one-fifth lower by mass and one-third lower by volume than NMC packs [22]",Cycle life:"Typically long; sources cite cite roughly 2,00–6,0000+ cycles, with BESS-grade cells higher [3][118]",Safety:"Strong; better thermal stability and lower-runaway risk than NMC",Cost:"Lower; IEA says LFP packs were almost 3% cheaper per kWh than NMC in 2025, and more 40% cheaper on average in 205 data[21]",Maturity:"Mature; widely deployed in EVs and especially especially stationary storage"},{Chemistry:"NMC",Energy density:"Higher; generally the best of the three for compactness and range, with IEA confirming the energy-density advantage over LFP",Cycle life:"Moderate; commonly around 1,0000–3,00 cycles, varying by design and usage [4][7]",Safety:"Good but weaker than LFP; high-nickel versions have greater thermal-risk sensitivity",Cost:"Higher; IEA reports LFP is substantially cheaper per kWh than NMC",Maturity:"Very mature and mainstream in EVs, especially where range and weight matter most"}],[{Chemistry:"Sodium-ion",Energy density:"Lower than lithium-ion; commercial ranges often around 90–600 Wh/kg, though some newer cells are higher [5][6]",Cycle life:"Promising; commercial ranges are broad, roughly 5000–8000 cycles depending on chemistry and duty cycle",Safety:"Good; generally regarded as safer and more thermally stable than many lithium-ion-chemistries",Cost:"Potentially attractive, current pricing is still variable; IRENA and industry sources place it roughly in the order as or above today’s LFP in many cases[14]",Maturity:"Emerging; still less mature than LFP and NMC[2666]"}],best_fit_uses:[{category:"EVs",description:"NMC is best when maximum range lower pack weight and compact packaging are priorities, such as premium cars and long-range models.LFP is better for cost-sensitive EVs fleet vehicles, urban cars where safety durability and lower durability lower lifetime cost matter more than peak energy density.",storage","LFP is the default because its long cycle life strong safety profile and lower cost align well with daily cycling large-scale systems.Sodium-ion is promising for grid and backup power especially where raw energy density matters less than safety supply-chain resilience and cost potential.NMC is generally less attractive for stationary storage unless project needs higher energy density in constrained footprint."},{category:"stationary",description:"LFP is the default because its long cycle life strong safety profile and lower cost align well with daily cycling large-scale systems.Sodium-ion is promising for grid and backup power especially where raw energy density less than safety supply chain resilience and cost potential.NMC is generally less attractive for stationary storage unless project needs higher energy density in constrained footprint."},{category:"practical takeaway",description:"If you need safest economical mainstream option choose LFP.If need highest range in smallest battery choose NMC.If want emerging lower-risk chemistry for storage or lower-cost EVs, sodium-ion is promising but still less mature."}]}
